<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE_SET>
<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE><EXPERIMENT accession="SRX26242090" alias="cDNA1"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRX26242090</PRIMARY_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>sediment metagenome supplemented with 25% atmospheric air (~4% oxygen)</TITLE><STUDY_REF accession="SRP535804"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP535804</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject">PRJNA1163300</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></STUDY_REF><DESIGN><DESIGN_DESCRIPTION>RNA was successfully extracted from the two incubated samples that had added laminarin. RNA was sequenced at the Joint Genomes Institute (JGI) according to standard methods</DESIGN_DESCRIPTION><SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR accession="SRS22783357"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS22783357</PRIMARY_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_NAME>cDNA1</LIBRARY_NAME><LIBRARY_STRATEGY>WGS</LIBRARY_STRATEGY><LIBRARY_SOURCE>METATRANSCRIPTOMIC</LIBRARY_SOURCE><LIBRARY_SELECTION>RANDOM</LIBRARY_SELECTION><LIBRARY_LAYOUT><PAIRED/></LIBRARY_LAYOUT></LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR></DESIGN><PLATFORM><ILLUMINA><INSTRUMENT_MODEL>NextSeq 2000</INSTRUMENT_MODEL></ILLUMINA></PLATFORM></EXPERIMENT><SUBMISSION lab_name="Deep Biosphere Laboratory" center_name="Bigelow Laboratory For Ocean Sciences" accession="SRA1977633" alias="SUB14743893"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRA1977633</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="Bigelow Laboratory For Ocean Sciences">SUB14743893</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>Bigelow Laboratory For Ocean Sciences</Name><Address postal_code="04544"><Department>Deep Biosphere Laboratory</Department><Institution>Bigelow Laboratory For Ocean Sciences</Institution><Street>60 Bigelow Dr</Street><City>East Boothbay</City><Sub>Maine</Sub><Country>USA</Country></Address><Contact email="tdangelo@bigelow.org" sec_email="timdangelo1@gmail.com"><Address postal_code="04544"><Department>Deep Biosphere Laboratory</Department><Institution>Bigelow Laboratory For Ocean Sciences</Institution><Street>60 Bigelow Dr</Street><City>East Boothbay</City><Sub>Maine</Sub><Country>USA</Country></Address><Name><First>Timothy</First><Last>D'Angelo</Last></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="BioProject" alias="PRJNA1163300" accession="SRP535804"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP535804</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA1163300</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Laminarin stimulates single cell rates of sulfate reduction while oxygen inhibits transcriptomic activity in coastal marine sediment</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>A vital part of benthic ecology are the chemical cycles carried out by bacteria and archaea living in coastal sediments. Within these ecosystems, the availability of carbon and sources of reducing and oxidizing power that fuel these sediment microbes can be subject to physical disruption, which may allow for increased respiration and consumption of complex carbon molecules - thereby impacting the cycling of nutrients in this environment often thought to be a terminal resting place. Here we describe the use of the redox-enzyme sensitive probe RedoxSensorTM Green to measure rates of electron transfer physiology in individual sulfate reducer cells that reside in anoxic sediment, which are subjected to transient exposure to oxygen and laminarin. We demonstrate a decoupling of respiration-related activity rates from transcription-derived activity, as oxygen exposure causes an increase in per cell respiration rate while overall decreasing the amount of RNA transcripts extractable from this anoxic community. Overall, we reveal an active community comprised primarily of respiring Chloroflexota and non-respiratory Patescibacteria that cycles sulfate and complex polysaccharides, important microbial aspects of this coastal sediment ecosystem.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="cDNA1" accession="SRS22783357"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS22783357</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN43889405</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|tdangelo" label="Sample name">cDNA1</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>749907</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>sediment metagenome</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>1163300</ID><LABEL>PRJNA1163300</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>collection_date</TAG><VALUE>2021-03-04</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>depth</TAG><VALUE>20 CM</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>elev</TAG><VALUE>-20 CM</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>env_broad_scale</TAG><VALUE>Marine</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>env_local_scale</TAG><VALUE>Intertidal Zone</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>env_medium</TAG><VALUE>Marine Sediment</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>geo_loc_name</TAG><VALUE>USA: Maine</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>lat_lon</TAG><VALUE>43.994827 N 69.6486 W</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>MIMS.me</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>MIGS/MIMS/MIMARKS.sediment</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES></SAMPLE><Pool><Member member_name="" accession="SRS22783357" sample_name="cDNA1" spots="71207924" bases="18912879803" tax_id="749907" organism="sediment metagenome"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS22783357</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN43889405</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></Member></Pool><RUN_SET runs="1" bases="18912879803" spots="71207924" bytes="6098433381"><RUN accession="SRR30843209" alias="cDNA1-A01_R1.fastq.gz" total_spots="71207924" total_bases="18912879803" size="6098433381" load_done="true" published="2024-10-01 09:22:22" is_public="true" cluster_name="public" has_taxanalysis="1" static_data_available="1"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRR30843209</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="SUB14743893" label="